## Yes, `==` can work reliably for string literals due to string interning, but...

### What is String Interning?

Java automatically interns string literals (strings defined directly in code using `"`). This means:

```java
String s1 = "hello";
String s2 = "hello";
String s3 = new String("hello");

System.out.println(s1 == s2);        // true - same interned object
System.out.println(s1 == s3);        // false - different objects
System.out.println(s1.equals(s3));   // true - same content
```

### Why `==` works for literals

When you write `"hello"`, Java stores it in the string pool (a special area in the heap). Any subsequent literal with the same content references the same object.

